    {
      "id": "TzsxCrcuxCTbY-ChDLcy2",
      "type": "text",
      "x": -1032.3300709781415,
      "y": 1653.5755417260943,
      "width": 1431.5789794921875,
      "height": 825,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1e1e1e",
      "backgroundColor": "transparent",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 1,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "b5T",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 2105322237,
      "version": 1295,
      "versionNonce": 1119671069,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": null,
      "updated": 1785693372455,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "*** Scaling DB horizontally\n1) Read replicas:\n- The key challenge is replication lag. When you write to the primary, it takes time to propagate to replicas.\n Users might not see their own changes immediately if they're reading from a lagging replica.\n\n\n2) DB sharding\n- Read replicas distribute load but don't reduce the dataset size that each database needs to handle. \nIf your dataset becomes so large that even well-indexed queries are slow, database sharding can help by splitting data across multiple databases.\n-- Functional Sharding (like different DB for different function - img)\n-- Geographic sharding: effective for global read scaling\n\n\n*** Application level caching (Redis/Memcached)\n- TTL: set fixed timeline for cached entries\n- Write-through invalidation: update/delet cache entries when writing to DB, ensure \nconsistency but add latency and error handling in write operation\n- Write behind invalidation: queue invalidation events to process asynchronously.\nReduce write latency but introduce window where stale data might be served\n- Tag invalidation: associate cache entries with tag, and invalidate entries\nwith specific tag. \n- Versioned keys: invlude version numbers in cache keys, and increment version on\nupdate, invalidte old cache entries\n\n*** CDN & Edge caching\n- only make senses for data accessed by multiple users, don't cache user specific data.\n(eg: public post, product catalog, search result, etc...)\n\n\n\n\n\n",
      "fontSize": 20,
      "fontFamily": 5,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "*** Scaling DB horizontally\n1) Read replicas:\n- The key challenge is replication lag. When you write to the primary, it takes time to propagate to replicas.\n Users might not see their own changes immediately if they're reading from a lagging replica.\n\n\n2) DB sharding\n- Read replicas distribute load but don't reduce the dataset size that each database needs to handle. \nIf your dataset becomes so large that even well-indexed queries are slow, database sharding can help by splitting data across multiple databases.\n-- Functional Sharding (like different DB for different function - img)\n-- Geographic sharding: effective for global read scaling\n\n\n*** Application level caching (Redis/Memcached)\n- TTL: set fixed timeline for cached entries\n- Write-through invalidation: update/delet cache entries when writing to DB, ensure \nconsistency but add latency and error handling in write operation\n- Write behind invalidation: queue invalidation events to process asynchronously.\nReduce write latency but introduce window where stale data might be served\n- Tag invalidation: associate cache entries with tag, and invalidate entries\nwith specific tag. \n- Versioned keys: invlude version numbers in cache keys, and increment version on\nupdate, invalidte old cache entries\n\n*** CDN & Edge caching\n- only make senses for data accessed by multiple users, don't cache user specific data.\n(eg: public post, product catalog, search result, etc...)\n\n\n\n\n\n",
      "autoResize": true,
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    },
