    },
    {
      "id": "j4xTAuCT-L7XPvqb2JxIs",
      "type": "text",
      "x": -1055.9789790686143,
      "y": 2394.7031113744406,
      "width": 1351.43896484375,
      "height": 1150,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1e1e1e",
      "backgroundColor": "transparent",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 1,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "b5a",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 447035453,
      "version": 1429,
      "versionNonce": 401900765,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": null,
      "updated": 1785693928650,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "1/ Queries slow down as data grows\n-- Normally caused by DB performing full table scan in column that is not indexing\n==> Add indexes based on actual query patterns\n- avoid over indexing since indexes increase storage and write cost\n- index in critical paths and denormalize in expensive join\n\n2/ Million of reads for hot cache keys\n- Option1: Request coalescing -- only 1 request per application serves load the missing value. Other request wait for same promise/future\n- Option2: Hot key replication or fanout -- store same value under several keys\n-- higher memory usage, more complex invalidation, replica may temporarily diverge, hot key detection and dynamic replication add complexity\n\n3/ Cache stampede after expiration\n- Option1: one request rebuilds the cache, other wait or use satle data ==> might block request behind one rebuild create latency spike\n- Option2: stale while revalidate -- keep serving old data while revalidate asynchronously\n\n-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nStep 1: Optimize the database\n* Fix query patterns.\n* Add indexes.\n* Eliminate N+1 queries.\n* Select only required columns.\n* Add pagination.\n* Inspect execution plans.\n* Denormalize critical paths where appropriate.\n\nStep 2: Scale database reads\n* Add read replicas.\n* Route non-critical reads to replicas.\n* Account for replication lag.\n* Separate transactional and analytical workloads.\n* Partition or shard only when necessary.\n\nStep 3: Add caching\n* Start with cache-aside.\n* Define TTL based on consistency requirements.\n* Protect cache misses with request coalescing.\n* Add stale-while-revalidate.\n* Detect and replicate hot keys.\n* Add CDN and edge caching for globally popular content.\n\nStep 4: Operationalize\n* Monitor hit rate, latency, eviction rate, hot keys, stale reads, and rebuild failures.\n* Test cache failure behavior.\n* Add load shedding and rate limiting.\n* Ensure the database can survive partial cache failure.\n* Define consistency requirements per data type.",
      "fontSize": 20,
      "fontFamily": 5,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "1/ Queries slow down as data grows\n-- Normally caused by DB performing full table scan in column that is not indexing\n==> Add indexes based on actual query patterns\n- avoid over indexing since indexes increase storage and write cost\n- index in critical paths and denormalize in expensive join\n\n2/ Million of reads for hot cache keys\n- Option1: Request coalescing -- only 1 request per application serves load the missing value. Other request wait for same promise/future\n- Option2: Hot key replication or fanout -- store same value under several keys\n-- higher memory usage, more complex invalidation, replica may temporarily diverge, hot key detection and dynamic replication add complexity\n\n3/ Cache stampede after expiration\n- Option1: one request rebuilds the cache, other wait or use satle data ==> might block request behind one rebuild create latency spike\n- Option2: stale while revalidate -- keep serving old data while revalidate asynchronously\n\n-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nStep 1: Optimize the database\n* Fix query patterns.\n* Add indexes.\n* Eliminate N+1 queries.\n* Select only required columns.\n* Add pagination.\n* Inspect execution plans.\n* Denormalize critical paths where appropriate.\n\nStep 2: Scale database reads\n* Add read replicas.\n* Route non-critical reads to replicas.\n* Account for replication lag.\n* Separate transactional and analytical workloads.\n* Partition or shard only when necessary.\n\nStep 3: Add caching\n* Start with cache-aside.\n* Define TTL based on consistency requirements.\n* Protect cache misses with request coalescing.\n* Add stale-while-revalidate.\n* Detect and replicate hot keys.\n* Add CDN and edge caching for globally popular content.\n\nStep 4: Operationalize\n* Monitor hit rate, latency, eviction rate, hot keys, stale reads, and rebuild failures.\n* Test cache failure behavior.\n* Add load shedding and rate limiting.\n* Ensure the database can survive partial cache failure.\n* Define consistency requirements per data type.",
      "autoResize": true,
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    },
    {
      "id": "yJ37b4ujtPxBZyhAl38pk",
      "type": "text",
      "x": 850.8224176385938,
      "y": -147.72497973264808,
      "width": 466.5396423339844,
      "height": 175,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1e1e1e",
      "backgroundColor": "transparent",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 1,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "b5b",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 1933997565,
      "version": 258,
      "versionNonce": 1532169821,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": null,
      "updated": 1785694020763,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "Scale write include\n1. Vertical scaling and DB choices\n2. Sharding and partitioning\n3. Handling bursts with queue and load shedding\n4. Batching and hierrachical aggregation\n\n",
      "fontSize": 20,
      "fontFamily": 5,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "Scale write include\n1. Vertical scaling and DB choices\n2. Sharding and partitioning\n3. Handling bursts with queue and load shedding\n4. Batching and hierrachical aggregation\n\n",
      "autoResize": true,
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    },
    {
      "id": "xIyuRAFdxW0cx4P1hFLTA",
      "type": "text",
      "x": 808.9686134788886,
      "y": 74.87210516450159,
      "width": 1248.718994140625,
      "height": 2025,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1e1e1e",
      "backgroundColor": "transparent",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 1,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "b5c",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 1474237139,
      "version": 1675,
      "versionNonce": 416649075,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": null,
      "updated": 1785700183319,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "*** Vertical Scaling & DB choices ***\n- Adding more hardware for vertical scaling is not the first choice, write are bottlenecked by disk I/O, CPU, network bandwidth\n\n--- DB Choices\n- Cassandra iw write-heavy database, achieves superior write throughput through its append-only commit log architecture\n- Cassandra  read is not great, reading requires multiple files and merging results, can be slower than well indexed relational DB\n- Time-series databases like InfluxDB or TimescaleDB are built for high-volume sequential writes,\n with timestamps (they also have built-in delta encodings to make better use of the storage)\n- Log-structured databases like LevelDB append new data rather than updating in place\n- Column stores like ClickHouse can batch writes efficiently for analytics workloads\n\n\n*** Sharding and Partioning ***\n\n** Horizontal Sharding\n\n- Hash key determine slot number, look up\nthe server responsible for that slot, and sends \nthe write request to the server\n\n- Select a good partitioning so data can be spread\nevenly across cluster => minimize variance \nin number of write per shard. \n\n\n** Vertical partitioning (eg: by function)\n\n-- Core post content (write-once, read-many)\nTABLE post_content (\n    post_id BIGINT PRIMARY KEY,\n    user_id BIGINT,\n    content TEXT,\n    media_urls TEXT[],\n    created_at TIMESTAMP\n);\n\n-- Engagement metrics (high-frequency writes)\nTABLE post_metrics (\n    post_id BIGINT PRIMARY KEY,\n    like_count INTEGER DEFAULT 0,\n    comment_count INTEGER DEFAULT 0,\n    share_count INTEGER DEFAULT 0,\n    view_count INTEGER DEFAULT 0,\n    last_updated TIMESTAMP\n);\n\n-- Analytics data (append-only, time-series)\nTABLE post_analytics (\n    post_id BIGINT,\n    event_type VARCHAR(50),\n    timestamp TIMESTAMP,\n    user_id BIGINT,\n    metadata JSONB\n);\n\n\n\n*** Handling burst with queues and load shedding ***\n\n** Write queues for burst handling with Kafka/SQS\n-- Decouple write accpetance from write processing,\nallowing system to handle write asynchronously.\n-- QUeue act as buffer, smoothing out traffic spikes\nDB process write at steady rate while queue handle bursts\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n** Load shedding strategies\n-- Problem such as Strava or Uber where statusneed to update in \nregular interval. => Drop least useful write during times of\nsystem overload. \n",
      "fontSize": 20,
      "fontFamily": 5,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "*** Vertical Scaling & DB choices ***\n- Adding more hardware for vertical scaling is not the first choice, write are bottlenecked by disk I/O, CPU, network bandwidth\n\n--- DB Choices\n- Cassandra iw write-heavy database, achieves superior write throughput through its append-only commit log architecture\n- Cassandra  read is not great, reading requires multiple files and merging results, can be slower than well indexed relational DB\n- Time-series databases like InfluxDB or TimescaleDB are built for high-volume sequential writes,\n with timestamps (they also have built-in delta encodings to make better use of the storage)\n- Log-structured databases like LevelDB append new data rather than updating in place\n- Column stores like ClickHouse can batch writes efficiently for analytics workloads\n\n\n*** Sharding and Partioning ***\n\n** Horizontal Sharding\n\n- Hash key determine slot number, look up\nthe server responsible for that slot, and sends \nthe write request to the server\n\n- Select a good partitioning so data can be spread\nevenly across cluster => minimize variance \nin number of write per shard. \n\n\n** Vertical partitioning (eg: by function)\n\n-- Core post content (write-once, read-many)\nTABLE post_content (\n    post_id BIGINT PRIMARY KEY,\n    user_id BIGINT,\n    content TEXT,\n    media_urls TEXT[],\n    created_at TIMESTAMP\n);\n\n-- Engagement metrics (high-frequency writes)\nTABLE post_metrics (\n    post_id BIGINT PRIMARY KEY,\n    like_count INTEGER DEFAULT 0,\n    comment_count INTEGER DEFAULT 0,\n    share_count INTEGER DEFAULT 0,\n    view_count INTEGER DEFAULT 0,\n    last_updated TIMESTAMP\n);\n\n-- Analytics data (append-only, time-series)\nTABLE post_analytics (\n    post_id BIGINT,\n    event_type VARCHAR(50),\n    timestamp TIMESTAMP,\n    user_id BIGINT,\n    metadata JSONB\n);\n\n\n\n*** Handling burst with queues and load shedding ***\n\n** Write queues for burst handling with Kafka/SQS\n-- Decouple write accpetance from write processing,\nallowing system to handle write asynchronously.\n-- QUeue act as buffer, smoothing out traffic spikes\nDB process write at steady rate while queue handle bursts\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n** Load shedding strategies\n-- Problem such as Strava or Uber where statusneed to update in \nregular interval. => Drop least useful write during times of\nsystem overload. \n",
      "autoResize": true,
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    },
