    {
      "id": "2kZ5jRUpYijMRc7Y0PssG",
      "type": "text",
      "x": 2518.206794304095,
      "y": 2211.3889629199693,
      "width": 743.5794067382812,
      "height": 200,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1e1e1e",
      "backgroundColor": "transparent",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 1,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "b5p",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 879417587,
      "version": 204,
      "versionNonce": 461236787,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": null,
      "updated": 1785701752236,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "<==> Better version, hierrachical aggregation\n\n- write processors aggregate the likes on comment they own, over a window,\nand forward a batch of updates to root processor.\n\n\n\n",
      "fontSize": 20,
      "fontFamily": 5,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "<==> Better version, hierrachical aggregation\n\n- write processors aggregate the likes on comment they own, over a window,\nand forward a batch of updates to root processor.\n\n\n\n",
      "autoResize": true,
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    },
    {
      "id": "sD9bnxAaTKeLBEFaqqCt2",
      "type": "text",
      "x": 890.5568968581138,
      "y": 2641.624134733065,
      "width": 903.87939453125,
      "height": 1275,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1e1e1e",
      "backgroundColor": "transparent",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 1,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "b5q",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 294460317,
      "version": 485,
      "versionNonce": 1605401085,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": null,
      "updated": 1785703219226,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "1) Reshard without downtime through gradual migration\n1. Provision the new shards\n2. Mark a key range as migrating\n3. Copy existing data from the old shard to the new shard\n4. Dual-write new updates to both shards\n5. Read from the new shard, with fallback to the old shard\n6. Verify data consistency\n7. Switch ownership to the new shard\n8. Stop writing to the old shard\n9. Delete old data after a safety period\n\n2) Dual write strategy\nWrite -> old shard + new shard\nRead -> new shard first, old shard as fallback\n\nThe same write may be retried or delivered to both shards multiple times. Writes should use:\n\n* Unique operation IDs.\n* Upserts.\n* Version numbers.\n* Idempotency keys.\n\n3) Dynamic Hot-key splitting: split only keys that exceed traffic threshold\nAdvantages\n* Normal keys remain simple.\n* Additional storage and read cost applies only to hot keys.\n* The split factor can increase as traffic grows.\n\nChallenges\n* Detecting hot keys.\n* Communicating split metadata.\n* Coordinating readers and writers.\n* Safely transitioning from one key to many keys.\n* Merging or retiring sub-keys after traffic falls.\n\n4) Safe transition from one key to multiple keys\n1. Mark key as SPLITTING\n2. Continue writing to the original key\n3. Begin dual-writing to the new sub-keys\n4. Copy or initialize the existing value\n5. Verify the aggregate\n6. Mark the split as ACTIVE\n7. Readers aggregate only the sub-keys\n8. Stop writing to the original key\n\nKey could be split: likes/views/impression/metrics, etc...\nKey could be hard to split: user_id, account_id, unique atomic entities\n\n\n\n",
      "fontSize": 20,
      "fontFamily": 5,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "1) Reshard without downtime through gradual migration\n1. Provision the new shards\n2. Mark a key range as migrating\n3. Copy existing data from the old shard to the new shard\n4. Dual-write new updates to both shards\n5. Read from the new shard, with fallback to the old shard\n6. Verify data consistency\n7. Switch ownership to the new shard\n8. Stop writing to the old shard\n9. Delete old data after a safety period\n\n2) Dual write strategy\nWrite -> old shard + new shard\nRead -> new shard first, old shard as fallback\n\nThe same write may be retried or delivered to both shards multiple times. Writes should use:\n\n* Unique operation IDs.\n* Upserts.\n* Version numbers.\n* Idempotency keys.\n\n3) Dynamic Hot-key splitting: split only keys that exceed traffic threshold\nAdvantages\n* Normal keys remain simple.\n* Additional storage and read cost applies only to hot keys.\n* The split factor can increase as traffic grows.\n\nChallenges\n* Detecting hot keys.\n* Communicating split metadata.\n* Coordinating readers and writers.\n* Safely transitioning from one key to many keys.\n* Merging or retiring sub-keys after traffic falls.\n\n4) Safe transition from one key to multiple keys\n1. Mark key as SPLITTING\n2. Continue writing to the original key\n3. Begin dual-writing to the new sub-keys\n4. Copy or initialize the existing value\n5. Verify the aggregate\n6. Mark the split as ACTIVE\n7. Readers aggregate only the sub-keys\n8. Stop writing to the original key\n\nKey could be split: likes/views/impression/metrics, etc...\nKey could be hard to split: user_id, account_id, unique atomic entities\n\n\n\n",
      "autoResize": true,
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    }
  ],
